1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,000
You will now hear a conversation between a university professor and a student about the essential characteristics and differentiating features of a literary review.

2
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:17,000
First, you have some time to look at questions 21 to 25.

3
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:56,000
Now, listen to the first part of the conversation and answer questions 21 to 25.

4
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:07,000
Hello. Good morning, Anna. How are you and how is that literary review coming along? Are you still working on it?

5
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:14,000
Hello, sir. I'm doing okay. Thank you for asking. Yes, I'm still working on it and that's why I'm here. I'd like to talk to you about it.

6
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:21,000
I'm drawing a blank on how to get started. I was, um, well, maybe you could suggest something to get me going.

7
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:29,000
Certainly. You do know that you must write a literary review. Have you ever written one and do you have any idea on how you plan to go about it?

8
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:35,000
Well, I know it's a literary review, but I don't really know what it really is or how to go about it.

9
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:44,000
I've written one before. I would imagine that, well, you take a book such as a novel or a book of poetry, settle into an easy chair and then read it.

10
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:51,000
Once you've finished it, you either give it thumbs up or thumbs down. Isn't that how a literary review is done, right?

11
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:57,000
Actually, no, that's not how it's done and that's just too simple. Also, that doesn't really mean anything.

12
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:02,000
If you really like a book or dislike it, you should be able to state why you like or dislike it.

13
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:09,000
You might also find that there are parts of a book that you like, but overall you might dislike it and vice versa.

14
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:17,000
Now, the nature of a literature review refers to any collection of materials on a particular topic and not necessarily the great literary texts of the world.

15
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:25,000
Now, what am I talking about here? Literature in and of itself could be anything from a set of government pamphlets on the colonial methods of the British in Africa

16
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:31,000
to scholarly articles on the treatment of a torn ligament. It could also entail fiction, prose or anything in between.

17
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:39,000
Furthermore, when you write a review, it does not mean that your reader wants you to give your personal opinion on whether or not you like the book or text.

18
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:46,000
Before you hear the rest of the conversation, you have some time to look at questions 26 to 30.

19
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:23,000
Now listen to the second part of the discussion and answer questions 26 to 30.

20
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:34,000
So what is a literary review?

21
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:44,000
A literature review discusses published information in a particular subject area and sometimes information of a particular subject area within a certain time period.

22
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:53,000
A literature review can be just a simple summary of the source, but it usually has an organizational pattern and combines both summary and synthesis.

23
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:58,000
OK, but how is a literature review different from an academic research paper?

24
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:07,000
The main focus of an academic research paper is to develop a new argument. A research paper will contain a literature review as one of its parts.

25
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:14,000
In a research paper, you use the literature as a foundation and as support for a new insight that you might contribute.

26
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,000
The focus of a literature review, however, is to summarize and synthesize the arguments and ideas of others without adding your own contributions.

27
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:27,000
OK, I see. I wonder about the purpose of writing a literature review.

28
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:31,000
I'm glad you brought that up because it's exactly what I wanted to talk about next.

29
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:36,000
You see, literature reviews provide you with a handy guide to a particular topic.

30
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:43,000
If you have limited time to conduct research, literature reviews can give you an overview or act as a stepping stone.

31
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:48,000
For professionals, there are useful reports that keep them up to date with what is current in their field.

32
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:54,000
For scholars, the depth and breadth of the literature review emphasizes the credibility of the writer in his or her field.

33
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:59,000
Literature reviews also provide a solid background for a research paper's investigation.

34
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:04,000
Comprehensive knowledge of the literature of the field is essential to most research papers.

35
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:07,000
But I'm just curious about who writes these.

36
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:13,000
Literature reviews are written occasionally in the humanities, but mostly in the sciences and social sciences.

37
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:17,000
Within experiments and lab reports, they constitute a section of the paper.

38
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:20,000
Sometimes a literature review is written as a paper in itself.

39
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:24,000
Well, I think it is now going to be easier for me to write the review.

40
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:28,000
Good. But don't forget that you have to submit it by the end of the class on Monday.

41
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:31,000
I'll be sure to do that. Thank you for your help.

42
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:35,000
That is the end of Part 3. You now have half a minute to check your answers.

43
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:11,000
